From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 09:19:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(model): a model that can park is parked where it would
 be stopped for room

A backend that can park a model answers the Free RPC by moving the model's
weights from the device to host memory and keeping everything else: its
process, its tokenizer, whatever it validated at the load. A LoadModel for
the model it holds, with the options of its load, brings them back (the
llama.cpp backend does both, for a model on context on demand whose
weights can leave the device). Coming back then costs a copy of the
weights, where a model that was stopped costs a load.

LocalAI stops models to make room - the memory reclaimer, the limit of
loaded models, a concurrency group - and until now that was the only way
to give a device back. A model whose file says `park: true` is PARKED
there instead, and brought back by the next request to it.

What decides a park, each time:
- the model's file says `park: true`. Such a model is also no longer sent
  Free on its way to being STOPPED: for it Free is not a prelude to a
  kill - it would copy the weights to host memory for a process about to
  die - and stopping the process frees the device, as for every backend
  that has no Free;
- a host memory floor is set (the runtime setting park_host_floor_bytes,
  0 by default: nothing is parked). Parked weights are host memory, and
  the host is not what it was an hour ago: its available memory is read
  before EACH park, and the model is parked only if the weights fit above
  the floor at that moment. Otherwise it is stopped, as before;
- the backend says what parking would move (Status, "park_size") and
  answers Free with success and "parked".

A busy model is never stopped for being busy at that moment: the eviction
keeps it and takes another.

A parked model:
- stays in the list of loaded models - it has a live process - but is not
  one of them for the watchdog: not counted against the limit, not an
  eviction candidate, not idle- or busy-checked, until it is back;
- comes back at the next request to it, before the request goes: it is a
  load for the limits (the models it may not share the device with are
  evicted or parked first), then one LoadModel with the options of its
  load. /backend/load does the same, for a caller that knows what comes
  next;
- if the device has no room for it then, the request is refused and the
  model stays parked.

For a caller that manages the device itself:
- POST /backend/park {"model"} parks a loaded model. It never waits and
  never stops anything; when the model is not parked the answer says why
  and the caller decides: 409 model_busy (take another), 412 cannot_park /
  parking_off / host_memory_floor (stop it instead, if room is still
  wanted), 404 not loaded;
- GET /system says, for a model that parks, "parks": true and "state":
  "loaded" or "parked", and for a parked one "parked": {"size_host",
  "size_vram_needed", "since"}. size_vram keeps its meaning: what the
  process holds on the device now. A model that does not park is listed
  as it always was.

"The device has no room now" is a 503. A backend's refusal of the moment -
gRPC ResourceExhausted whose message begins "no_room_on_device: " - was a
500 like any fault. It is answered 503 with Retry-After and the type
no_room_on_device, on every route, and as an error event of that type when
it comes in the middle of a chat stream. The code alone is not enough:
ResourceExhausted is also a stream that is busy and a message over the
size limit, which stay what they were. The embeddings route no longer
rewrites such a refusal as "conversation too long".

Tests: the loader against a scripted backend (25 specs: what parks, what
does not and why, the host read at each park, the return with the options
of the load, a refused return, a failed one, the stop that sends no Free);
the watchdog (parks before it stops, never stops for busy); the endpoint,
/system and the 503 mapping. A model that does not park: every existing
spec of pkg/model, core/config, core/backend and core/schema passes.

